  "@type": "Capability",
  "critical": true,
  "description": "Industrial electrolysis cell driving non-spontaneous reactions via DC current between two electrodes in an electrolyte. Anode oxidizes species, cathode reduces them; operating voltage exceeds the thermodynamic reversible potential by overpotentials and ohmic losses. Three chlor-alkali designs: diaphragm cells (asbestos or polymer separator, simplest, tolerates impure brine), membrane cells (ion-exchange membrane, most energy-efficient, requires ultra-pure brine), and mercury cells (liquid cathode, phased out for pollution). Also covers alkaline water electrolysis for hydrogen production. Produces chlorine, hydrogen, and NaOH simultaneously; among the most electricity-intensive industrial equipment.",
